{
  // An OrbStack VM running incus as a COI (Code on Incus) host. Set explicitly by
  // pickled-coi's vm/setup.sh, never auto-detected: nothing at runtime
  // distinguishes this VM from a laptop, so dotpickles_detect_role's early
  // return (functions.sh) relies on DOTPICKLES_ROLE already being set in the
  // environment. This is a placeholder so DOTPICKLES_ROLE=coi-host resolves to
  // a real role file and doesn't trip claudeconfig.sh's fail-loud missing-role
  // guard (ADR 0031).
  //
  // Agent identity: deliberately reuses the home agent identity rather than
  // minting a coi-host one. This VM is an extension of the user's personal
  // machine, and a separate identity would need its own GitHub enrollment on
  // every VM rebuild, which fights the rebuild-from-scratch goal this VM
  // exists to prove. The private key is copied into the VM by pickled-coi's
  // vm/auth.sh (copying was chosen over the alternatives).
  //
  // See claude/roles/home.jsonc for the full GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL explanation.
  "env": {
    "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL": "~/.gitconfig.d/claude-agent-home",
  },

  // No other sandbox overrides yet. Add rules here if a concrete COI-host
  // workflow needs them -- don't copy home's sandbox block as-is, its
  // allowWrite is a macOS Library path and its allowedDomains are Mac-specific.

  // Auto mode: this VM has no org/cloud context of its own, so base.jsonc's
  // "None configured" picture is otherwise fine here, same as under home.
  // But claudeconfig.sh only ever merges base + ONE role, so home.jsonc's
  // bullets don't reach a session that starts with DOTPICKLES_ROLE=coi-host
  // -- the one thing worth carrying over is the dev-VM-vs-production
  // distinction, since a session here could just as easily `ssh picklelab`
  // or run a `just deploy-*` as one on the laptop. Duplicated from
  // home.jsonc rather than shared (no cross-role include mechanism exists);
  // keep the two in sync if this changes. See ADR 0056.
  "autoMode": {
    "environment": [
      "### Org-wide",
      "**Sensitive remote targets**: any namespace, host, or container whose name carries `prod` or `production` as a whole word or name segment; also `picklelab` (personal homelab host, reached over Tailscale) and anything deployed there via the `picklehome` repo, even though the name itself doesn't say \"prod\"",
      "**Protected deployment namespaces / environments**: the personal homelab — the `picklelab` host and whatever `picklehome` (`~/github.com/technicalpickles/picklehome` on the Mac, public) deploys onto it: home automation with physical-world effects (HVAC via `climate/`, the garage door via `garage/`) plus always-on app services like `brineworks-server` (see that repo's `docs/dev-vs-prod-pipeline.md`). Real production — treat `ssh picklelab`, and any `docker compose`/`just deploy-*`/`just climate-*`/`just garage-*` command that mutates state there, with the same caution as any other prod system",
      "### User-specific",
      "this VM (pickled-coi) is itself the disposable dev sandbox that mirrors most of technicalpickles/ repos under its own `~/projects/` -- ordinary development here is not a remote-host escalation. `picklelab`/`picklehome` (above) is different: real infrastructure, not something to experiment on freely, even though it's one `ssh` away like anything else",
    ],
  },
}
